ROLE: Act as a code agent. Make exactly one change, plus its test.

Problem: in `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs`, the check `non-interactive-permission-mode-risk` (near the end of `CHECKS`) applies only when the command matches `/\bclaude\s+-p\b|\bclaude\s+--print\b/`, so the print flag must come straight after `claude`. `claude --model sonnet -p "x"` is still a Claude print-mode dispatch (`inspectDispatch` and the `HEADLESS_DISPATCH_SHAPES` Claude entry both accept flags before `-p`), yet the check returns `true` and the packet's warning never fires.

Fix: make the check's applicability test use the same Claude print-mode shape `HEADLESS_DISPATCH_SHAPES` already uses, `/\bclaude\s+(?:[^|;&]*\s)?(?:-p|--print)\b/`. Define it once as a named constant beside `PI_PRINT` (for example `CLAUDE_PRINT`), with a one-line comment, and use it in both places: the `HEADLESS_DISPATCH_SHAPES` entry and the check. Keep the bypass test (`--dangerously-skip-permissions` or `--permission-mode bypassPermissions`) as it is.

Test: in `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.test.mjs`, add one `test(...)` near 'other checks discriminate correctly' that asserts, through `CHECKS['non-interactive-permission-mode-risk']`:
- `claude --model sonnet -p "x"` returns `false`;
- `claude --model sonnet --print "x"` returns `false`;
- `claude --model sonnet -p --dangerously-skip-permissions "x"` returns `true`;
- `claude --model sonnet --permission-mode bypassPermissions -p "x"` returns `true`;
- `claude -p "x"` still returns `false`, and `claude --version` returns `true`.
Import `CHECKS` only if the file does not already.

Comments keep the durable WHY and never name spec paths, phases, or task, review or finding ids.

